                <h2>Context: Three Efficiency Killers in Agency Account Opening</h2>
<p>The biggest challenge in ad agency account opening isn't getting accounts approved—it's clients constantly asking for updates, missing documents, and accounts mysteriously suspended.</p>
<p>These problems share a common root: <strong>no systematic process management tool</strong>. When monthly account openings grow from 10 to 50, spreadsheets, WeChat groups, and memory alone can't keep up.</p>
<p>A systematic kanban isn't a fancy management concept—it's a practical tool for making repetitive work controllable.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Checklist: Do These Problems Exist in Your Account Opening Service?</h2>
<p>Before building a kanban, confirm your current pain points:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Multiple progress versions across different channels (email/WeChat/call)</li>
<li>[ ] No visibility into which stage documents are stuck at</li>
<li>[ ] Delayed client notifications after account top-ups, causing campaign pauses</li>
<li>[ ] Inconsistent service quality depending on individual experience</li>
<li>[ ] Abnormal account spending discovered during monthly reconciliation with no audit trail</li>
</ul>
<p>If you checked more than 2 items, your account opening service needs systematic improvement.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Design Basic Kanban Columns</h2>
<p>The core of kanban is "account status visibility." Design columns based on your workflow:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Status</th>
<th>Meaning</th>
<th>Trigger</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Documents Pending</td>
<td>Client inquired but hasn't submitted complete credentials</td>
<td>New ticket created</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Internal Review</td>
<td>Documents submitted, awaiting initial internal check</td>
<td>Operations receives documents</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Platform Submission</td>
<td>Account application submitted to Meta/Google/TikTok</td>
<td>Entering platform review</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Additional Materials</td>
<td>Platform requires supplementary documents</td>
<td>Platform feedback received</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Account Approved</td>
<td>Account activated, ready for top-up</td>
<td>Account confirmation received</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Active Management</td>
<td>Account delivered, ongoing service</td>
<td>Client completes first top-up</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Dormant</td>
<td>No spending for 30+ days, needs activation or archiving</td>
<td>Regular check triggers</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip</strong>: More columns aren't better. Over 7 columns increase cognitive load. Prioritize core workflow visibility.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Configure Action Lists for Each Column</h2>
<p>Kanban should not only show status but also drive status transitions. Each column has specific actions:</p>
<p><strong>Internal Review</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Cross-check document completeness against platform requirements</li>
<li>[ ] Verify consistency of business license, legal representative, and advertiser website</li>
<li>[ ] Flag uncertainties in ticket notes for client confirmation</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Additional Materials</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Record platform's reason for requesting additional materials (save screenshots)</li>
<li>[ ] Notify client within 24 hours with required document list</li>
<li>[ ] Set deadline reminder (recommend 2-day buffer)</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Account Approved</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Record account ID, name, and approval timestamp</li>
<li>[ ] Sync account info to financial system, establish top-up ledger</li>
<li>[ ] Send account delivery email with login guide and top-up instructions</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Set Up Automated Reminder Rules</h2>
<p>Manual tracking leads to gaps. Automated reminders are essential:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Document expiry alert</strong>: Warning when credential validity falls below 30 days</li>
<li><strong>Balance threshold alert</strong>: Notify client and operations when account balance drops below preset threshold (e.g., $500)</li>
<li><strong>Dormant account check</strong>: Trigger activation check for accounts with 14+ consecutive days of no spending</li>
<li><strong>Renewal reminder</strong>: Alert client 15 days before annual credential renewal</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Establish Financial Reconciliation Checklist</h2>
<p>Account opening service involves top-up and spending tracking. Link kanban to financial fields:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Link each top-up record to account ID, timestamp, amount, and payment method</li>
<li>[ ] Weekly reconcile platform backend balance with financial records</li>
<li>[ ] Flag abnormal spending (e.g., 200%+ daily increase) for investigation</li>
<li>[ ] Export account reports monthly, confirm spending with clients</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Post-Implementation Review and Iteration</h2>
<p>First week after kanban launch, verify effectiveness:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Count how many accounts completed the full kanban flow, identify any "stuck" statuses</li>
<li>[ ] Gather operations team feedback on redundant fields</li>
<li>[ ] Measure time from client status inquiry to operations lookup</li>
<li>[ ] Update standard operating documents (SOP) based on actual cases</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Summary: Kanban Transforms Account Opening from "Person-to-Person" to "System-Driven"</h2>
<p>The ultimate goal of systematic kanban isn't looking organized—it's achieving:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Improved client experience</strong>: Transparent process, clients can check progress anytime, reducing unnecessary follow-ups</li>
<li><strong>Improved operations efficiency</strong>: New team members can onboard quickly, expertise no longer exists only in individual heads</li>
<li><strong>Controllable financial risk</strong>: Top-ups and spending have records, anomalies are traceable</li>
</ul>
<p>Start with a simple kanban, get core statuses flowing first, then gradually add financial and collaboration features. This is a more feasible improvement path than "getting it perfect from day one."</p>
